1
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:06,000
Hello.

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:14,560
This time, using the human anatomy and simplified drawing methods we've learned so far, let's draw a trendy human body across the whole body.

3
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:20,940
We've been practicing a realistic body based on reality.

4
00:00:20,941 --> 00:00:23,740
Is a realistic body enough?

5
00:00:23,741 --> 00:00:26,460
For example, I'm a real person.

6
00:00:26,461 --> 00:00:32,220
If I drew pictures of my face and body, or made a game with them, would it sell?

7
00:00:33,580 --> 00:00:37,020
Sadly, I don't think anyone would buy it.

8
00:00:37,021 --> 00:00:40,821
Because my body and face aren't trendy.

9
00:00:40,939 --> 00:00:44,380
So what is a trendy body?

10
00:00:44,381 --> 00:00:47,740
Trendy, in its dictionary sense, means fashionable.

11
00:00:47,741 --> 00:00:50,860
Saying a fashionable body sounds a bit odd.

12
00:00:50,861 --> 00:00:59,180
But since I do commercial art, I've worked hard to draw a body that the largest number of unspecified people would prefer.

13
00:00:59,181 --> 00:01:19,800
For example, for a man, an inverted-triangle back, a strong chest, and chocolate abs, plus long and sturdy limbs. And for a woman, a small head, a full chest, a firm waist, a wide pelvis, and long, extended limbs.

14
00:01:19,801 --> 00:01:22,440
Especially, subtle flows matter.

15
00:01:22,440 --> 00:01:25,960
Of course, personal taste is also included.

16
00:01:25,961 --> 00:01:29,880
I want to let you know upfront that I can't speak for everyone's taste.

17
00:01:33,924 --> 00:01:37,600
I've drawn what I think is a trendy, normal male body.

18
00:01:37,601 --> 00:01:39,520
Let's compare it with a bad example.

19
00:01:41,449 --> 00:01:44,040
Let me start by comparing a skinny body type.

20
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:45,240
Can you see the difference?

21
00:01:45,240 --> 00:01:49,560
This right sketch is also based on a realistic body.

22
00:01:49,561 --> 00:01:51,400
It's a drawing of a realistically thin man.

23
00:01:52,119 --> 00:01:55,560
But being too realistic makes it less appealing.

24
00:01:55,561 --> 00:01:59,640
This sketch on the left could be called a model fit.

25
00:01:59,640 --> 00:02:01,720
Do more people prefer the left?

26
00:02:01,721 --> 00:02:04,840
Or do more people prefer the right?

27
00:02:04,841 --> 00:02:08,919
As people who do commercial design, we always have to work for the choice of the majority.

28
00:02:09,560 --> 00:02:15,841
Even if the right body type is realistic and to my taste, if the public doesn't see it that way, it's meaningless.

29
00:02:16,390 --> 00:02:20,200
That would only make sense if you were drawing just for your own satisfaction.

30
00:02:20,201 --> 00:02:25,480
For game concept art, not many people would want to play as a character like that.

31
00:02:25,481 --> 00:02:28,881
You can see that just being realistic isn't what matters.

32
00:02:29,295 --> 00:02:31,000
Let me look at other body types too.

33
00:02:31,589 --> 00:02:37,240
Even for a muscular man, you have to draw him more attractively in a drawing.

34
00:02:37,241 --> 00:02:42,100
In real life, this body type would look very sturdy and cool.

35
00:02:42,101 --> 00:02:44,340
But in a drawing, it doesn't have much competitive edge.

36
00:02:44,341 --> 00:02:46,180
Because it can be more trendy.

37
00:02:48,250 --> 00:02:51,420
In the seventies, there was a movie called The Hulk.

38
00:02:51,421 --> 00:02:55,820
The one on the right is drawn after the Hulk's body from that movie.

39
00:02:55,821 --> 00:02:59,180
It was such an old movie that I haven't seen it, but I've seen stills.

40
00:02:59,744 --> 00:03:03,020
He was an actor with an amazing body even without CGI.

41
00:03:03,021 --> 00:03:08,140
But I couldn't imagine that body appearing in a modern Hulk movie.

42
00:03:08,141 --> 00:03:13,180
Because we've gotten so used to the Hulk's body exaggerated with CGI.

43
00:03:13,181 --> 00:03:21,700
In this way, rather than being limited to just realism, we should base ourselves on reality and exaggerate the appealing elements as needed.

44
00:03:23,075 --> 00:03:27,150
For a muscular combat-ready body, you have to draw the muscle lines even more alive.

45
00:03:28,810 --> 00:03:37,320
And for a skinny body, you have to draw it cleanly so that the muscle lines stay alive while giving a sleek image.

46
00:03:41,935 --> 00:03:43,880
Is it enough to just make the body trendy?

47
00:03:43,881 --> 00:03:44,120
No.

48
00:03:44,310 --> 00:03:50,120
Since we have to capture attention with a single image, composition and perspective are important too.

49
00:03:50,121 --> 00:03:53,800
Let's learn about the composition and perspective that make a drawing attractive.

50
00:03:55,640 --> 00:04:01,171
A viewpoint can move along the X-axis, Y-axis, and depth axis like this.

51
00:04:01,959 --> 00:04:07,610
Many people draw as if zooming in from far away along the depth axis.

52
00:04:08,225 --> 00:04:12,490
For example, like a photo taken with a zoom from far away during a sports broadcast.

53
00:04:13,530 --> 00:04:17,370
Even though the person is far back, their head can look bigger.

54
00:04:17,371 --> 00:04:18,810
Because it doesn't feel like it has perspective.

55
00:04:21,655 --> 00:04:26,960
A drawing that looks like it's zoomed in from far away is easy to draw but less appealing.

56
00:04:28,310 --> 00:04:33,880
So I prefer a drawing that looks like it's zoomed out at a moderate distance.

57
00:04:34,900 --> 00:04:38,161
It's the distance of when a person actually meets another person.

58
00:04:38,845 --> 00:04:43,000
No one meets people by zooming in with a telescope from far away.

59
00:04:44,380 --> 00:04:45,470
It's the power of familiarity.

60
00:04:46,245 --> 00:04:51,310
This kind of realistic viewing distance can make a drawing more realistic.

61
00:04:52,990 --> 00:04:57,200
And next, the axis to pay attention to is the Y-axis.

62
00:04:57,201 --> 00:05:00,681
If you're a game concept artist, you should pay attention to this Y-axis.

63
00:05:00,750 --> 00:05:01,840
It's called the over-the-top view.

64
00:05:02,480 --> 00:05:08,030
There are quite a lot of games that look like they're looking down from above like this.

65
00:05:08,031 --> 00:05:13,390
When designing, there are many times you need to design so it looks effective when looked at from above like this.

66
00:05:13,470 --> 00:05:15,230
The same goes for drawing concept art.

67
00:05:16,190 --> 00:05:22,990
It's good to place the observer's viewpoint, that is, the camera position, slightly above the center of the character.

68
00:05:22,991 --> 00:05:26,270
You can show the top design in a focused way.

69
00:05:26,271 --> 00:05:28,030
The top of the foot is visible too.

70
00:05:28,031 --> 00:05:30,590
You should avoid low angles.

71
00:05:30,591 --> 00:05:34,770
Conversely, a picture that looks like it's shot from below along the Y-axis gives the character power.

72
00:05:35,600 --> 00:05:37,810
It's said to feel overwhelming.

73
00:05:37,811 --> 00:05:40,210
It's used when showing character personality.

74
00:05:41,685 --> 00:05:46,190
If the perspective exceeds this too much, you can't use it as a concept.

75
00:05:46,191 --> 00:05:47,950
Modelers hate it.

76
00:05:47,951 --> 00:05:54,630
Especially, in illustration, if you use this viewpoint and composition well, you can draw even more attractive pictures.

77
00:05:58,615 --> 00:06:01,920
Let's find out through practice.

78
00:06:01,921 --> 00:06:04,640
I've drawn a character here.

79
00:06:04,641 --> 00:06:09,200
It's a picture with almost no perspective, like it was shot zoomed in from far away.

80
00:06:10,780 --> 00:06:15,191
Let me redraw this with a trendy body and composition.

81
00:06:17,075 --> 00:06:21,580
In most cases, I draw from the head first.

82
00:06:21,581 --> 00:06:23,100
It's a habit.

83
00:06:23,101 --> 00:06:29,861
Since I prefer drawings with a lot of perspective, the face usually ends up at a low angle.

84
00:06:31,210 --> 00:06:32,650
This picture is like that too.

85
00:06:34,730 --> 00:06:37,290
I draw quickly, mainly by silhouette.

86
00:06:40,545 --> 00:06:42,700
There's nothing to think of as hard.

87
00:06:42,701 --> 00:06:46,860
The advantage of digital art is that you can make unlimited corrections.

88
00:06:46,861 --> 00:06:47,900
As long as you have the will.

89
00:07:26,460 --> 00:07:30,300
Before I knew it, the skeleton was all set.

90
00:07:30,301 --> 00:07:32,380
I go about tidying while adding flesh.

91
00:10:07,039 --> 00:10:10,400
I said it's important to exaggerate the appeal points.

92
00:10:10,670 --> 00:10:14,521
I exaggerated the overall flow like this.

93
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:17,050
Compare it with the left character.

94
00:10:42,970 --> 00:10:43,970
If you do that.

95
00:11:13,264 --> 00:11:14,650
Let me compare the perspective.

96
00:11:16,765 --> 00:11:20,481
The left sketch has almost no perspective in it.

97
00:11:21,264 --> 00:11:23,530
It's stable but boring.

98
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:41,011
This right picture has relatively excessive perspective.

99
00:11:49,555 --> 00:11:53,461
The top is a low angle, but the bottom is a high angle.

100
00:11:54,280 --> 00:12:02,460
If you draw like this, the picture feels more three-dimensional, and if it feels three-dimensional, the character gets lively.

101
00:12:02,461 --> 00:12:04,380
This is mainly how I've been drawing.

102
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,850
Let me draw one more quickly.

103
00:12:09,851 --> 00:12:14,090
Practice drawing bodies of various body types in a trendy way.

104
00:17:46,564 --> 00:17:49,470
Shall we review the important body parts too?

105
00:17:49,471 --> 00:18:00,090
The collarbone, the sternocleidomastoid, and the pectoralis major.

106
00:18:12,640 --> 00:18:18,080
The external oblique, the latissimus dorsi, and the iliac crest.

107
00:18:23,234 --> 00:18:26,620
And the overall flow of the leg.

108
00:18:28,360 --> 00:18:29,630
I told you all that.

109
00:18:35,419 --> 00:18:36,510
And the flow of the arm.

110
00:18:39,039 --> 00:18:47,680
I said the flow of the arm and the brachioradialis, which starts at the upper arm bone and connects to the radius on the thumb side, are important.

111
00:18:53,770 --> 00:18:59,610
Let's also check the position of the navel.

112
00:18:59,611 --> 00:19:01,450
Let me do a few more.

113
00:19:01,451 --> 00:19:08,810
It might be my own taste, but I like a body that's long and slim yet firm.

114
00:19:08,811 --> 00:19:11,050
I'll also draw it while changing the body type.

115
00:23:42,870 --> 00:23:49,411
To make the lines of the body visible, I've let the arm drop away from the body to create a gap.

116
00:23:49,534 --> 00:23:56,211
In this way, you should be wary of elements colliding and design so the silhouette shows well.

117
00:24:55,480 --> 00:24:58,440
Let me do one more.

118
00:24:58,441 --> 00:25:01,000
This time, I'm going to draw a bunny girl.

119
00:25:01,001 --> 00:25:05,320
Likewise, I'll redraw the normal bunny girl on the left.

120
00:25:07,554 --> 00:25:12,400
If you ask why I copy-pasted something I'm going to fix anyway, I have nothing to say.

121
00:25:16,734 --> 00:25:19,000
This time, I'll draw it with more exaggeration.

122
00:25:19,669 --> 00:25:21,560
I'll pull the pelvis back.

123
00:25:22,364 --> 00:25:24,280
I've moved the ribcage forward.

124
00:25:28,304 --> 00:25:32,600
When I make corrections, I often use the lasso tool.

125
00:25:32,601 --> 00:25:35,841
It's not great to rely on it too much.

126
00:25:36,349 --> 00:25:38,590
But I can't tell you not to use it.

127
00:25:38,591 --> 00:25:39,591
Because it's so convenient.

128
00:25:41,155 --> 00:25:42,190
It's good for your mental health.

129
00:25:43,794 --> 00:25:46,590
With the lasso tool around, I always feel secure.

130
00:26:08,340 --> 00:26:13,240
I also draw while mirroring left and right.

131
00:26:13,241 --> 00:26:17,121
Honestly, this normal character isn't wrong.

132
00:26:18,110 --> 00:26:21,120
The point is to make it look a bit more appealing.

133
00:26:21,735 --> 00:26:23,200
Please watch while comparing.

134
00:26:25,850 --> 00:26:28,860
This is a very common pose.

135
00:26:28,861 --> 00:26:30,540
He could be holding a weapon.

136
00:26:32,124 --> 00:26:36,440
But having the arm cover the upper body like this isn't good in concept art.

137
00:26:38,009 --> 00:26:44,501
Since this picture has a simple design, and it's not going to be made into a 3D model, I drew it like this.

138
00:26:57,314 --> 00:27:02,460
This isn't a coloring chapter, but let me do a simple color and wrap up.

139
00:27:05,984 --> 00:27:09,781
I'll explain coloring in detail in a later chapter.

140
00:27:13,149 --> 00:27:17,801
I'm not going to finish it, just a first-pass color.

141
00:27:38,300 --> 00:27:42,460
After rendering the image, I merge the layers and I'm in the middle of rendering.

142
00:27:46,754 --> 00:27:51,950
Characters like this have no pain in the design, so I like that I can draw them lightly.

143
00:27:53,764 --> 00:27:57,481
How about we draw a bunny girl this evening, everyone?

144
00:28:04,989 --> 00:28:08,510
I keep rendering at a zoomed in view.

145
00:28:08,511 --> 00:28:11,671
It's important to render the whole thing.

146
00:28:14,324 --> 00:28:19,630
I didn't notice when drawing, but watching the video, the face was gradually getting weird.

147
00:28:24,094 --> 00:28:28,810
Actually, at that time too, the face kept bothering me, so I kept touching it up.

148
00:28:47,449 --> 00:28:55,100
If a face isn't pretty, you lose the urge to draw.

149
00:28:55,180 --> 00:29:00,300
If you find a part you don't like like this, boldly erase it and draw it again.

150
00:29:02,539 --> 00:29:06,211
Holding onto it just doesn't seem to give you an answer.

151
00:29:18,429 --> 00:29:22,791
I slightly turned the pose to redraw the face.

152
00:29:35,199 --> 00:29:37,520
Let me wrap up the coloring about here.

153
00:29:41,975 --> 00:29:48,080
In this chapter, we looked at an efficient figure drawing method and trendy drawing.

154
00:29:48,081 --> 00:29:54,160
Trendiness can be subjective, so I won't impose my taste on you.

155
00:29:54,161 --> 00:30:00,960
Just think of it as advice from a senior or colleague who has worked in the industry for a long time.

156
00:30:00,961 --> 00:30:04,361
In Chapter One, I talked about study methods, right?

157
00:30:04,604 --> 00:30:11,720
I hope you'll think about and judge for yourself what trendiness is, and take an active approach to learning.

158
00:30:13,134 --> 00:30:20,600
Next chapter, before character coloring, let's take time to learn about light and color.

159
00:30:20,601 --> 00:30:21,601
Great work.
